## The version contract

You are writing against one pinned Blender series. Its API is the only
API. Code that would work on another series is broken code here.

The dominant failure mode in this work is not bad geometry — it is
calling an API that moved. Measured on 212 object categories across 12
models, roughly 85% of the failures in the two worst-performing models
were 4.x to 5.0 drift: a removed BSDF socket, a renamed mesh attribute,
an enum that no longer exists, a keyword that was replaced. Those models
were not modelling-capacity-limited, they were knowledge-cutoff-limited.
You are subject to the same limit and cannot feel it from the inside.

So:

- Use the operations listed for you. That list is complete and current.
  Reading it costs nothing; guessing costs a turn and a traceback.
- Never reach for a raw operator when a listed operation exists. The
  listed ones are context-free and pinned; raw operators take their
  arguments and their behaviour from a context you did not set.
- If you find yourself recalling a signature rather than reading one,
  stop. That recollection is from a different version.

## Reading a traceback

A traceback that names an attribute, a key, an enum member or a keyword
is a drift report, not a logic error. Do not re-derive your approach
around it — look up the current name and substitute it.

When a traceback teaches you a drift you did not know, say so plainly in
your answer, with the old name, the new name and the version that moved
it. That sentence is worth more than the fix: the fix repairs one
script, the sentence repairs every future one.

The same error twice the same way is not a retry, it is a loop. Stop and
report what you are stuck on.
